/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  /* Land 1. Tiny origin, large ask, pinned at the centre. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    base_size 1
    land_percent 30
    land_position 50 50
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

  /* Land 2. Declared after, pinned at the SAME place, with a stamp large enough
     to bury land 1's square completely, and almost no ask of its own. */
  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    base_size 8
    land_percent 1
    land_position 50 50
    border_fuzziness 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_53 - what does the engine do with a land whose starting square is
covered by a later land before it grows?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.1, lands.ts, Sec.15 item 30, and item 27

QUESTION
Sec.6.1 stamps every land origin first and grows every land afterwards, and
growth's frontier is defined as tiles next to ones the land already owns. A
later land's stamp can cover an earlier land's square completely, and that
earlier land then owns nothing, has no frontier, and grows nothing at all.

This is not hypothetical. It is the cause of ALL FIFTEEN empty lands on the
corpus, found while measuring something else. One official map lays a forest
ring four tiles apart at a base size that makes the stamps overlap by design -
the ring looks perfect and nothing is missing from the picture - and another
loses a land declaring thirty-four thousand tiles this way.

The preview currently says so out loud with a note rather than marking anything
on the canvas, because a marker there would blame the author for our
approximation.

THREE MODELS FIT AND NOTHING SEPARATES THEM YET
  (a) The engine behaves as we do and the land is genuinely lost. The note is
      over-cautious but harmless and Sec.6.1 stands.
  (b) The engine grows from the origin POSITION rather than from owned tiles, so
      a covered land expands out from under its neighbour and appears as a ring
      or a crescent around it.
  (c) The engine grows lands IN SEQUENCE rather than stamping all origins first.
      Then the earlier land is already fully grown before the later one is
      stamped, and the question does not arise in this form - what needs
      correcting is the stamp order in Sec.6.1, not the growth rule.

(c) WOULD ALSO BEAR ON ITEM 27, which is why this run is worth more than its own
question. That item is about where a fragmented land's detached seeds are
allowed to land, and a sequential pipeline changes what "already occupied" means
for every land after the first.

METHOD
Two lands at the same pinned position. The first asks for a large share of the
map from a tiny origin. The second is declared after it and has a stamp big
enough to bury the first one's square completely, but asks for almost no growth
of its own.

  (a) -> NO snow anywhere. Only the dirt stamp, roughly the size it was stamped.
  (b) -> Snow appears as a ring or crescent AROUND the dirt, growing outward
         from under it. Snow area well short of the asked-for share.
  (c) -> A LARGE snow region, close to the asked-for share, with a dirt patch
         sitting on top of its middle.

The three outcomes differ in snow area by roughly nothing, a little, and
everything, so this does not need a careful instrument.

WHY THE FIRST LAND ASKS FOR SO MUCH
So that (c) is unmistakable. If both lands asked for similar shares, (b) and (c)
would both give "some snow" and the reading would turn on a proportion. A large
ask makes (c) fill much of the map and (b) fill a collar.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. THREE RUNS. Three because (b) and
(c) both produce a snow region and the DISTINCTION is its size relative to the
ask, which varies run to run with what the growth happens to do.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT
  Snow area against the declared share is the reading. Also check whether the
  snow is a connected region containing the dirt, or a collar around it, or
  absent - the shape separates (b) from (c) even when the areas are close.
*/
